"<p>Under <strong>Data → Databases</strong>, Nitrobase lets you deploy standalone database servers backed by official Docker images, complete with automated backups, in-browser query consoles, and private app linking.</p>\n<h2>Supported Engines</h2>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n<table><thead><tr><th align=\"left\">Engine</th><th align=\"left\">Default Version</th><th align=\"left\">Available Versions</th><th align=\"center\">Default Port</th><th align=\"center\">Console</th><th align=\"center\">User / DB Management</th><th align=\"left\">In-Place Upgrade Policy</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td align=\"left\"><strong>PostgreSQL</strong></td><td align=\"left\"><code>18.6</code></td><td align=\"left\"><code>18.6</code>, <code>17.11</code>, <code>16.15</code>, <code>15.19</code>, <code>14.24</code></td><td align=\"center\"><code>5432</code></td><td align=\"center\">SQL (<code>psql</code>)</td><td align=\"center\">Yes (with DB owners)</td><td align=\"left\">Patch within major (<code>redeploy</code> rollback)</td></tr><tr><td align=\"left\"><strong>MySQL</strong></td><td align=\"left\"><code>8.4.11</code></td><td align=\"left\"><code>8.4.11</code>, <code>9.7.2</code></td><td align=\"center\"><code>3306</code></td><td align=\"center\">SQL (<code>mysql</code>)</td><td align=\"center\">Yes</td><td align=\"left\">Patch within major (<code>redeploy</code> rollback)</td></tr><tr><td align=\"left\"><strong>MariaDB</strong></td><td align=\"left\"><code>11.8.9</code></td><td align=\"left\"><code>11.8.9</code>, <code>11.4.13</code>, <code>10.11.19</code></td><td align=\"center\"><code>3306</code></td><td align=\"center\">SQL (<code>mariadb</code>)</td><td align=\"center\">Yes</td><td align=\"left\">Forward (<code>restore</code> rollback)</td></tr><tr><td align=\"left\"><strong>Valkey</strong></td><td align=\"left\"><code>9.1.2</code></td><td align=\"left\"><code>9.1.2</code>, <code>9.0.6</code>, <code>8.1.10</code></td><td align=\"center\"><code>6379</code></td><td align=\"center\">Redis (<code>valkey-cli</code>)</td><td align=\"center\">Single password</td><td align=\"left\">Forward (<code>restore</code> rollback)</td></tr><tr><td align=\"left\"><strong>MongoDB</strong></td><td align=\"left\"><code>8.0.32</code></td><td align=\"left\"><code>8.0.32</code>, <code>9.0.2</code></td><td align=\"center\"><code>27017</code></td><td align=\"center\">JSON (<code>mongosh</code>)</td><td align=\"center\">Default user/DB</td><td align=\"left\">Patch within major (<code>redeploy</code> rollback)</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<div class=\"docCallout docCallout-note\"><div class=\"docCalloutHeader\"><span class=\"docCalloutBadge\">NOTE</span><span class=\"docCalloutTitle\">Network Privacy by Default</span></div><div class=\"docCalloutContent\">\n<p>Standalone databases are <strong>not</strong> routed through Traefik and run on an <code>internal</code> Docker network by default. If you need external access, explicitly enable the published host port under <strong>Settings → Published ports</strong> (or connect over an SSH tunnel).</p>\n</div></div>\n<h2>Linking Databases to Apps</h2>\n<p>Instead of exposing database ports publicly, link a database directly to any app on the same server:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Open the database and go to the <strong>Links</strong> tab (or link from the app's settings).</li>\n<li>Nitrobase creates an isolated bridge network (<code>nitrobase-link-&#x3C;id></code>) and attaches only the database container and the linked app's containers by container name (so a database named <code>db</code> never collides with an app's own <code>db</code> service).</li>\n<li>Nitrobase injects connection environment variables automatically—by default the connection URL (<code>DATABASE_URL</code>, <code>REDIS_URL</code>, or <code>MONGODB_URL</code>) and optionally individual parts (<code>PGHOST</code>/<code>PGPORT</code>/etc., <code>MYSQL_*</code>, <code>REDIS_*</code>, <code>MONGODB_*</code>).</li>\n<li>For engines with user management (PostgreSQL, MySQL, MariaDB), you can link apps using a dedicated least-privilege login or the main login (requires <code>database.write</code>).</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>Built-in Web Console</h2>\n<p>Every database includes an audited web console in the dashboard:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>SQL Console (PostgreSQL / MySQL / MariaDB):</strong> Runs in read-only mode by default inside a <code>READ ONLY</code> transaction with a 15-second statement timeout (<code>DATABASE_CONSOLE_TIMEOUT_MS</code>) and 500-row limit (<code>DATABASE_CONSOLE_MAX_ROWS</code>). Write statements require <code>database.write</code> permission.</li>\n<li><strong>Command Console (Valkey / MongoDB):</strong> Runs one command or JSON command document per execution (<code>SCAN</code> instead of <code>KEYS</code> in read-only mode for Valkey).</li>\n<li><strong>Rate &#x26; Concurrency Limits:</strong> Up to 2 concurrent console runs and 60 runs per minute per member/token.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Backups &#x26; Upgrades</h2>\n<p>Each engine uses its native consistent backup strategy (<code>postgres</code> cluster dump, <code>mysql</code>, <code>mariadb</code>, <code>redis</code> RDB snapshot, <code>mongodb</code> archive). Engine and version are stored as <code>&#x3C;engine>@&#x3C;tag></code> (e.g. <code>postgres@18.6</code>) so backups, restores, clones, and server moves preserve the exact version.</p>"